\title{MO-3: eager reduction on the machine as the official procedure (the mode bit in place, local pair entries)}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{soundness}
\tag{performance}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Package "MO-3" of 2026-11-25 (21:30 UTC to the early hours of the next UTC day). The maintainer's instruction of that day: the re-check by the reference engine is turned off in the default configuration, the machine is to follow the official behaviour by itself, and the reference engine is a fallback for development that must not affect production. So the machine has to decide every declaration that it hands to the reference engine today. This package takes one class of them, \strong{the declines of eager reduction}: a declaration that the machine declines because it applies \code{eagerReduce}. Its question was why the machine's switch \code{eager} ([the design note](design-eager-reduction), [the report of the package that built it](rep-eager-reduction)) speculates on what eager reduction would do instead of doing what the official kernel does, and whether an exact mechanism without probes, without a second run and without declines exists. It does, and it is built: a patch for the module \code{rlvm-machine}, not applied and not published. The work was done in private copies of the tracked sources, the official verdicts are those of leanchecker 4.36.0 in Docker, and every timing was taken in windows of \src{bench/quiet.py}.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Result in one page}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Why the machine probed.} Not because it could not carry the flag. It always carried it the official way: one bit, \code{Machine.ER}, set around the one comparison of an \code{\verb~|eagerReduce _ _~} argument's type with its domain and read at the two places where the official kernel reads its flag. What it could not do was let the two modes share its tables as the official kernel shares its caches: the official caches live for one declaration, the machine's pair table lives for a session, so a plain answer of an earlier declaration answered a comparison under the flag that the official kernel computes, and computes differently. The first mechanism therefore moved the eager comparisons to the end of the declaration and probed, at every point of the plain phase where the modes could part, whether they do; where they did, or where a probe was cut, it declined.}
\li{\strong{What has to be keyed.} Not the mode: the official caches are shared by the two modes, and a table partitioned by mode rejects what the official checker accepts (a pair compared in plain mode and then under the flag in one declaration is answered from the cache). What an entry needs is the \em{life} of an official cache entry, one declaration - and only if its answer can depend on the mode at all. An entry written by a comparison that passed no point where the modes may part is the same in both modes and stays what it was, an entry of the session.}
\li{\strong{The mechanism} (section 3). The comparison runs where it stands, under the bit. A comparison that passes a point where the modes may part is marked, and what a marked comparison writes into the pair table is a \em{local} entry: it answers in both modes, in its own declaration only. Under the bit the machine runs the official order without the shortcuts of plain mode. Three rules keep the machine from rejecting what the official cache would have answered: a failure under the bit after an eager-only event is looked up in the pair table by expression, the K rule asks the plain comparison before it leaves a recursor stuck, and a declaration is checked again alone before it is rejected. No probe, no deferred comparison, nothing declined.}
\li{\strong{Declines.} No declaration of the eight corpora is declined for eager reduction: con-leche 1 unit, cslib 4 and Mathlib 4 before, 0 after (the counter \code{machine.eagerDeclines} counts the driver's cold retry as well: 2, 8, 8). The 230 published arena tests had none before and have none.}
\li{\strong{Hostile exports.} The suite's eager family (55 rows) and 46 new rows with official verdicts (31 accept, 15 reject): the order of the two modes inside one binder, across binders and across declarations, nested applications, the application as a constructor argument, as a let value and with a third argument, a pair behind closure congruence and behind a proof position, a recursion that only the eager order evaluates, the K rule with two proofs. In the default configuration the machine alone gives the official verdict on 93 of the 101 rows and accepts the other 8, all of kind T (a derivable statement that the official checker rejects through its eager order or its cache): four by the K rule, three by a binder instance, one behind a shortcut of plain mode. It declines none, accepts no row that is not of kind T and rejects no official accept. The published mechanism gives the official verdict on 63, accepts 37 and declines 1. On the 1,274 rows the suite had before, 16 of the 74 kind T accepts of the published machine become the official verdict (section 5.2).}
\li{\strong{Cost.} In the machine's ticks the mechanism is cheaper than the published one on every corpus (-0.02 to -1.25 \%: no second run in a fresh session, no probe), and the local entries cost -0.03 to +0.04 \% against the same build with shared entries (section 6). In warm wall clock with 8 workers nothing measurable changes: the guard of [the rule for a default](dec-YP0N) passes on both launchers, geometric mean 0.996 on the executable and 0.993 on the JVM launcher, no corpus above 1.011, every ratio inside the spread of its rounds; the peak resident set is unchanged (section 7).}
\li{\strong{Found on the way.} A first version of the mechanism rejected an export that the official checker accepts; it was found by a hostile row of this package before any gate, reported at once and repaired (section 5.3). It is the reason for the third of the three rules above.}
\li{\strong{Verdict.} \strong{GO} for the mode \code{local} as what the switch \code{eager} does in the default set, with two conditions. No declaration of the eight corpora or of the arena tests is declined for eager reduction any more; the machine alone gives the official verdict on 93 of the 101 hostile rows of the family and accepts the other 8, each a derivable statement with a named cause; no gate shows a wrong accept or a wrong reject; the differential engine finds no unit on which the machine and the reference engine differ on the eight corpora; the guard passes on both launchers. The conditions: an adversarial pass judged by the official checker, which [the standing policies](standing-policies) ask for before a switch enters a default set and which a switch of that set should get again when it changes what it does - the points are section 11, and with a rejection of the machine now final the first of them (the run alone as the only net under a rejection) is the one that matters; and a merge by hand with the package on the K rule after a let application, which a mechanical merge gets wrong (section 12). The mode \code{deferred} stays in the sources as the fallback and is the published mechanism to the tick.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. Where the official kernel's behaviour depends on the flag}

\p{The official source is \code{\verb~|src/kernel/type_checker.cpp~} of Lean 4.36.0 (the reading of the kernel is [the study of the official type checker](study-kernel-typechecker)). The flag is the field \code{\verb~|m_eager_reduce~} of the type checker. It occurs four times.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Where}\th{What}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|infer_app~}, check mode only}\td{\strong{Set.} If the ARGUMENT of the application is of the form \code{\verb~|eagerReduce _ _~} - the constant of that name applied to exactly two arguments, looked at as the term stands - the flag is true for the extent of the one comparison of the argument's type with the domain of the function's type, and is restored after it. The type of the argument itself is inferred before, without the flag.}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|lazy_delta_reduction~}}\td{\strong{Read.} The Nat folding of a pair (\code{\verb~|reduce_nat~} of one side, then of the other; if one folds to a literal, the answer of the whole comparison is the comparison of that literal with the other side) is tried if neither side has a free variable, \em{or the flag is set}.}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|is_def_eq_core~}}\td{\strong{Read.} The test against \code{\verb~|Bool.true~} on the right (the left side is reduced to weak head normal form and the answer is true if that is \code{\verb~|Bool.true~}) is made if the left side has no free variable, \em{or the flag is set}.}}
\tr{\td{the declaration of the field}\td{false at the start of every declaration.}}
}

\p{Consequences that the hostile rows of section 5 confirm with official verdicts. A let value \code{\verb~|let h : T := eagerReduce S p~} is compared with its declared type in plain mode: only \code{\verb~|infer_app~} sets the flag. An application \code{\verb~|eagerReduce S f x~} with a third argument is not of the form and is compared in plain mode; its inner part \code{\verb~|eagerReduce S f~} is the function of an application, not an argument. An argument that is itself an \code{eagerReduce} application inside another one is compared under the flag like any other. A metadata node around the application hides it (the loader's rule, section 9). Comparisons under the flag do not nest: the comparison never calls check-mode inference.}

\p{Nothing else reads the flag. Reduction and inference depend on the mode in exactly one way: the K rule (\code{\verb~|to_cnstr_when_K~}) asks a comparison, of the type of the major premise with the type of the constructor application, and its answer decides whether the recursor reduces. Everywhere else the answer of a comparison is the answer of a comparison.}

\p{\strong{The caches.} The success cache of comparisons, the failure cache of same-head argument comparisons, the two reduction caches and the two inference caches belong to one type checker, which is one declaration. They are keyed by terms, whose free variables are unique per binder instance, and they are read and written in both modes: a pair that succeeded in plain mode answers true under the flag before anything is computed, and the reverse. So the official answer to a comparison is not a function of the pair and the mode. It is the answer of the mode that asks, unless the declaration has already answered the same pair of terms.}

\p{\strong{The reference engine} is a port of this: \code{\verb~|RefKernel.eagerReduce~} is set and restored around the comparison in its \code{infer_app}, and read at the Nat folding of its lazy delta loop and at its two \code{\verb~|Bool.true~} tests; its caches are term-keyed and per declaration.}

\p{\strong{The machine} had the same bit from its first version: \code{\verb~|Machine.ER~}, set through \code{\verb~|Machine.enterER~} by the two places that check an argument (\code{\verb~|Infer.twD~}, and \code{\verb~|Code.targConv~} for elaborated code), saved before and restored after the comparison, by the continuation frame if the comparison is suspended and by the cut of a speculative segment; read by \code{\verb~|Conv.lazydFold~} and \code{\verb~|Conv.convBoolTrue~}, the two official places. The machine-only memo of a false comparison is neither read nor written under the bit. That part needed no change.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. Why the machine probed, and what has to be keyed}

\p{The machine computes on hash-consed values with canonical free variables, and its memos live for a warm session: the pair table with its three status bits (\code{POS}, the success cache; \code{FAILED}, the failure cache; \code{NEG}, a memo of its own), the memo words of reductions and types on the value records. Shared between the modes like the official caches, they answer \em{more} than the official caches: a plain answer of an earlier declaration, or of another instance of a binder, answers under the flag, where the official kernel has nothing cached and computes with the eager order. The findings S-F4 and S-F5 of [the soundness review of the machine](rep-soundness-machine) are that: the pair \code{\verb~|X = Y p~} with \code{\verb~|X = Nat.add (G intro) 0~} and \code{\verb~|Y p = Nat.add (G p) 0~} is true in plain mode (same head, arguments compared, the proofs by proof irrelevance) and false under the flag (the closed side folds to 0 first, and 0 against the open side ends at a stuck recursor).}

\p{The first answer was to decline every declaration that enters eager reduction (the rule M6-F26). The second, the switch \code{eager} of package E1, kept such declarations by ordering the work: the eager comparisons are deferred to the end of the declaration, so that no plain judgment reads an answer derived under the flag, and the plain phase \em{probes}, at every point where the modes could part, what the eager branch would do - because whether a plain answer may be read under the flag depends on whether its two modes agree, and the plain procedure does not compute that. A probe evaluates what the official plain procedure never evaluates, so it needed a budget; a probe that is cut, a point with two answers, an eager-only event inside the K rule decline the declaration. That is the mechanism the declines of this package's class come from.}

\p{\strong{What has to be keyed.} The task suggested a mode bit in the comparison state with the memo tables keyed or partitioned by it. The bit is right and was there. The partition is not what the official kernel does: its caches are \em{shared} by the modes, and the hostile row \code{warm-pos-same-declaration-eager-ok} (one declaration needs the pair in plain mode and then under the flag; official verdict: accept, through the success cache) is rejected by any table that answers a comparison under the flag only from entries made under the flag. What differs between the official caches and the machine's tables is not the mode but the life. So an entry needs a life, and only an entry whose answer can depend on the mode needs it:}

\ul{
\li{a comparison that passes no point where the two modes may take different branches is the same computation in both modes; its entries are valid in both modes for as long as the machine's entries are valid at all;}
\li{a comparison that passes such a point has an answer of its own mode. The official kernel keeps that answer for the rest of the declaration and gives it to both modes. So must the machine: the entry is \em{local}, valid in both modes in the declaration that wrote it and absent afterwards.}
}

\p{That is a stamp on some entries of the pair table and nothing else. The memo words of reductions need no stamp if the one place where a comparison decides a reduction, the K rule, is made to answer the same in both modes (section 3, rule 6). The cost of the stamp is section 6: 0.00 to 0.04 \% of the machine's ticks.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The mechanism}

\p{The switch is still \code{eager}; how it keeps a declaration is a mode, \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-mode~}: \code{local} is the mechanism of this report and the default; \code{shared} is the same without local entries (rules 3 and 4 off: what partitioning buys and costs is the difference between the two); \code{deferred} is the first mechanism, unchanged, with its probes and its budget. Without the switch, and in a strict engine, a declaration that enters eager reduction is declined as before (M6-F26).}

\ol{
\li{\strong{In place.} The comparison of an \code{\verb~|eagerReduce _ _~} argument's type with its domain runs where it stands, under the bit, and a false answer is the official error of the application at that place. Nothing is deferred, so the order of plain and eager comparisons inside a declaration is the official order: a pair that is first asked under the flag is computed under the flag.}
\li{\strong{The official order under the bit.} The two shortcuts of plain mode that are not the official procedure, \code{congruence} (the same-head probe, closure congruence) and \code{skipargs} (skipped proof positions and unread positions), are not used while the bit is set: a same-head probe answers before the Nat folding of lazy delta, which is exactly where the eager order parts from the plain one, and a skipped proof position hides two proofs that the official kernel compares by their types under the flag. On the eight corpora no comparison under the bit used either shortcut: the machine's tick counts are the same with and without them there.}
\li{\strong{Marks.} A comparison is marked when it passes a point where the two modes may take different branches. In plain mode: an open pair a side of which a Nat folding may answer (a cheap test that looks at what is already known: an argument that would have to be a literal and is known not to be one rules the folding out), at the Nat folding of lazy delta and before the same-head probe and closure congruence answer such a pair; an open term against \code{\verb~|Bool.true~}. Under the bit: an eager-only event, that is, a folding or a \code{\verb~|Bool.true~} test that the bit alone allowed and that answered. A comparison that reads a local entry is marked, and a comparison inherits the mark of every comparison it asked. The mark is a mark of the comparison in progress, not of the declaration: the host frame of the comparison keeps the enclosing one's mark; if the comparison is suspended to the frame stack, that mark travels in a frame of its own, and the cut of a speculative segment gives it back. So the marks, and with them every pair entry, do not depend on the host depth bound; the machine's gate compares the entries under four bounds (section 8).}
\li{\strong{Local entries.} What a marked comparison writes into the pair table - a success, a failure of a same-head argument comparison, the memo of a false plain comparison - is a local entry: the status word carries a bit and the number of the declaration, and a reader in another declaration reads the entry as absent. Inside its declaration it answers in both modes, as an entry of the official caches does. An entry written by a comparison that is not marked is an entry as before: its status word is unchanged, bit for bit.}
\li{\strong{By expression.} The official success cache is keyed by terms, the pair table by value records, and one expression can be more than one record. When a comparison under the bit ends false in a declaration that has seen an eager-only event, the pair table is searched once more, by expression (the machine's structural identity of two values, which is the official \code{\verb~|is_equiv~}): an entry that a reader may use and whose two sides are the two expressions answers true. The search is bounded per declaration.}
\li{\strong{The K rule.} Under the bit, a comparison of the K rule that ends false after an eager-only event inside it is asked again in plain mode, and the recursor reduces if either answer is true. So a recursor reduces under the bit whenever it reduces in plain mode, a memo word of a reduction means the same in both modes, and no memo word needs a stamp. This is the one rule that is \em{not} the official procedure: where the comparison is true in plain mode and false under the flag, the official kernel leaves the recursor stuck unless its reduction cache holds the reduct from a plain reduction of the same term earlier in the declaration - which an engine on values cannot know, because it skips the comparison of two terms that are one value (the row \code{e1-skip-krule-plain-redex-then-eager-ok}). The reduction is valid either way; what the rule can add is the acceptance of a derivable statement (section 5.2).}
\li{\strong{Alone before a rejection.} The machine remembers which terms it has checked, in a warm session and across sessions in the shared check facts; the official kernel checks every term of every declaration anew and fills its caches doing so. Before the machine rejects a declaration in which an eager-only event happened, it checks the declaration again alone: in a fresh session and without the shared check facts, so that every plain comparison the official kernel makes in that declaration has been made by the machine in it, and stands in a local entry. The verdict of that run is the machine's. (Inside one declaration the machine's variables are canonical, and a term checked under one instance of a binder is not checked again under the next; that is why a local entry is not ended when a binder is opened again, section 5.2.)}
}

\p{What is gone in this mode: the deferred comparisons and the re-opening of binders, the probes and their budget, the second run of a declaration that reaches its first eager argument in a session with a mode-sensitive answer (109 to 1,437 runs per corpus, table of section 6), the forgetting of a session after an eager-only event, and every decline.}

\p{\strong{Soundness.} Every true answer of a comparison is a valid conversion in both modes: both orders are sound procedures, an entry with a success holds a valid conversion whatever mode wrote it, the search by expression answers for the same two expressions, and a reduction by the K rule that a plain comparison licensed is a valid reduction. The comparison of every eager argument is made, in its own scope, before the declaration is accepted - it is the same call the plain arguments go through. So the mode handling can change which derivable statements are accepted beyond the official checker, and never accept a statement that is not derivable.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. Conditions}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Builds.} Private copies of the tracked sources of the main tree, never the main tree itself: a pristine copy and a copy with the patch, each built with \src{scripts/build.sh} as a development distribution (the executable, a GraalVM Community Edition native image with the inline lists of the machine, and the JVM launcher with the jars). The first pair of copies was taken from the main tree of 21:33 UTC (the published build of 20:00 UTC, jars \code{70e7d9767d42} as a tree build); the second pair, on which every final gate and the guard ran, from the main tree of 22:46 UTC, which carries the patches of [the package on the re-check](rep-MO-1): a rejection of the machine is final by default. The default configuration throughout: the machine with its default switch set, no \code{-X} option unless a row names one.}
\li{\strong{Counts} are from one worker in file order (\code{\verb~|--threads 1 --stats -X stats-file=F~}) through the JVM launcher, where every counter of the machine is deterministic. They are not timings.}
\li{\strong{Timings} are warm passes that reload the file with pipelined loading, 8 workers (\code{\verb~|--threads 8 --stats -X passes=N~}, N = 10 on Init and Std, 8 on cedar, 15 on reachability, 6 on con-leche, navier-stokes-euler and cslib, 4 on Mathlib), the variants interleaved round by round inside one \src{bench/quiet.py} window per launcher on the 8 measurement cores of the aarch64 development host. A cell is the mean over three rounds of the median later pass of a process; for passes under 0.5 s the mean over the rounds of the mean later pass, as [the rule for a default](dec-YP0N) says.}
\li{\strong{Official verdicts}: leanchecker 4.36.0 (\code{\verb~|--from-export~}) in the project's arena image, without network, as [the gate](design-gate) takes them.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Hostile exports}

\subtree{
\title{5.1 The rows}

\p{The eager family of the suite (\src{rlvm-machine/src/test/resources/s/manifest.tsv}) is 55 rows: package S's, package E1's, the reviewer S3's and OPT-H's rows that enter eager reduction ([the suite](rep-machine-S), [the review of the default set](rep-soundness-default-set), section 10.1). This package adds 46 rows \code{\verb~|mo3-*~} (generator \src{rlvm-machine/src/test/resources/s/gen/batchM.py}, built with the suite's builder on the arena's prelude). Every official verdict was taken with leanchecker 4.36.0 in the project's Docker image, as the suite's are: 31 accept, 15 reject. Three of my expectations were wrong and are corrected by the official verdict; each taught something (marked * below). The building blocks are the suite's: \code{\verb~|X = Y p~} is the pair that is true in plain mode and false under the flag; \code{\verb~|Nat.add 0 1 = K1 p~} is a pair whose modes agree although the fold is an eager-only event.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Group}\th{Rows}\th{What is attacked, and the official verdicts}}
\tr{\td{order in one binder}\td{3}\td{plain, eager, plain (accept: the success cache); eager first, then plain twice (reject: nothing cached yet); a harmless eager-only event first, then the pair in plain mode, then under the flag (accept)}}
\tr{\td{binder instances}\td{8}\td{the plain comparison inside an inner binder that the pair does not mention, the eager one after it is closed (accept); the same with the pair under OTHER RECORDS - an argument written as a beta redex, so that the suspended redex is one record under the inner binder and another one outside it, while the official terms are one term (accept), and its twin under the flag alone (reject); the eager comparison under a further binder (accept), with the further variable in the pair (reject); two sibling binders at depth two, plain then eager (reject: another free variable), both plain (accept); the outer pair after two siblings were opened and closed (accept)}}
\tr{\td{across declarations}\td{6}\td{plain in one declaration, plain and eager in the next (accept: the cache of the second); plain, eager, plain in three declarations (reject at the second); a harmless eager declaration, the pair in plain mode, the eager one again, the pair again (accept); a definition whose value holds an open term that compares the pair in plain mode, then a theorem with the SAME term and the pair under the flag, in two forms (accept: section 5.3); an answer that was derived from the cached pair in one declaration, needed under the flag in the next (reject)}}
\tr{\td{behind closure congruence}\td{4}\td{the two sides written as beta redexes of one lambda, so that for an engine on values the arguments are one term under two frames whose slots are convertible proofs: plain (accept), under the flag alone (reject), plain in one declaration and eager in the next (reject), both in one binder (accept)}}
\tr{\td{nested}\td{4}\td{the proof inside the eager argument makes the plain comparison (accept: the argument is inferred before its type is compared); \code{eagerReduce} applied to \code{eagerReduce} with the false comparison inside (reject) and with true ones (accept); a plain application around a false eager comparison, the plain proof after it (reject)}}
\tr{\td{where the application stands}\td{7}\td{as the argument of a constructor, eager field first (reject), plain field first (accept); as a LET VALUE (accept: plain mode, only \code{\verb~|infer_app~} sets the flag); a let value that contains an eager argument (reject); a plain let value, the eager argument in the body (accept); with a THIRD argument (accept: not of the form, plain mode *: my first form of the row wrapped it in a second \code{eagerReduce} and was rejected for that); the mismatch in the function of the over-applied form (reject)}}
\tr{\td{the \code{\verb~|Bool.true~} test}\td{1}\td{the open term on the left, where the official test looks: accept}}
\tr{\td{a long recursion}\td{3}\td{the shape of the four units of \code{\verb~|Batteries.Data.Char.Basic~}: an open pair \code{\verb~|Nat.add (W p) 1 = Nat.add (W p) (Nat.succ 0)~} that plain mode answers by comparing arguments and that the eager order answers by folding, which evaluates \code{\verb~|W p~}, about 135,000 recursor steps. In plain mode beside an unrelated eager comparison (accept), under the flag (accept), plain then eager in one binder (accept)}}
\tr{\td{only the eager order?}\td{2}\td{\code{\verb~|1 = Nat.mod (K7 p) 3~} under the flag (accept) and in plain mode (accept): no comparison was found that is true under the flag and false in plain mode. With the official Nat functions the unfolding reaches what the folding reaches; what differs is the work (the row group above)}}
\tr{\td{behind a proof position}\td{6}\td{two applications that differ in a proof only, whose types are the pair: plain (accept); under the flag in the declaration whose own type holds the applications (accept *: the check of the type compared the two proof types in plain mode); the applications as two definitions, plain (accept), under the flag (reject), plain in one declaration and eager in the next (reject)}}
\tr{\td{the K rule}\td{3}\td{two recursors with two opaque proofs of the pair, compared under the flag: alone (accept: both stay stuck and are compared argument by argument), after a plain declaration that reduced one of them (accept), after a plain reduction in the same binder (accept *: the K comparison of both is then the cached pair of types, both reduce)}}
}

\p{The rows the task asked for by name: a comparison that succeeds only in plain order is the family's pair; one that succeeds only in eager order was not found (above); the same pair first under the flag and then without it, and the other way round, inside one declaration and across declarations: groups 1 and 3 and the family's \code{e1-benign-*}, \code{e1-warm-*} and \code{oh-eager-*} rows; nested, under a binder, as a constructor argument, as a let value, a literal-heavy recursion: groups 5, 2, 6 and 8.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.2 Results}

\p{The machine's own verdict, in process (the suite's harness: the real loader, the reference engine beside one machine lane), per mode of the switch. "Wave 6 with eager" is the default set without the two shortcuts of plain mode. The eager family, 101 rows (60 official accepts, 41 rejects):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Switch set}\th{Mode}\th{Official verdict}\th{Accept of a kind T row}\th{Declined}\th{Wrong accept}\th{Wrong reject}}
\tr{\td{default}\td{\code{local} (this report)}\td{93}\td{8}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{default}\td{\code{shared}}\td{86}\td{15}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{default}\td{\code{deferred} (the published mechanism)}\td{63}\td{37}\td{1}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{wave 6 with eager}\td{\code{local}}\td{94}\td{7}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{wave 6 with eager}\td{\code{shared}}\td{86}\td{15}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{wave 6 with eager}\td{\code{deferred}}\td{59}\td{0}\td{42}\td{0}\td{0}}
}

\p{The whole suite under the default set, 1,320 rows; in brackets the 1,274 rows that the suite had before this package:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Mode}\th{Official verdict}\th{Accept of a kind T row}\th{Declined}\th{Rejected, official accept (the twelve known rows of the plain cache history)}\th{Metadata row}}
\tr{\td{\code{local}}\td{1,241 (1,197)}\td{60 (58)}\td{6 (6)}\td{12}\td{1}}
\tr{\td{\code{shared}}\td{1,234 (1,194)}\td{67 (61)}\td{6 (6)}\td{12}\td{1}}
\tr{\td{\code{deferred}}\td{1,211 (1,180)}\td{89 (74)}\td{7 (7)}\td{12}\td{1}}
}

\p{\strong{So of the 74 rows of the earlier suite that the published machine accepts as kind T, 16 get the official verdict}, and the one row of the family it declined is accepted: \code{warm-eager-pair-alone-bad}, \code{warm-pos-across-declarations-eager-bad}, \code{warm-pos-same-declaration-eager-first-bad}, \code{canon-fvar-pos-across-binders-eager-first-bad}, \code{e1-eager-pair-alone-bad}, \code{e1-benign-then-bad-pair-eager-bad}, \code{e1-defer-false-then-plain-ok-bad}, \code{e1-nested-eager-inside-eager-bad}, \code{e1-site-in-axiom-type-bad}, \code{e1-site-in-definition-type-and-value-bad}, \code{e1-let-variable-under-binder-eager-bad}, \code{e1-bad-pair-eager-then-plain-other-binder-bad}, \code{e1-warm-plain-benign-then-eager-bad}, \code{s3-mdata-eager-plainform-bad}, \code{oh-eager-lam-eager-alone-bad}, \code{oh-eager-lam-plain-then-eager-bad}; and \code{e1-krule-benign-eager-alone-ok}. (The figure 78 of the launcher gate counts the pipeline's verdicts, the launcher rows are in section 8.) The six rows that are still declined are not declines of eager reduction: the loader's metadata rule (two rows, and the metadata row of the family), the string-literal prerequisites, and three rows of the K rule after a let application.}

\p{\strong{The eight rows of the family that the machine accepts and the official checker rejects} carry the new note \code{eager-accept} in the manifest, each with its cause (section 10): four by the K rule, three by a binder instance, one by a shortcut of plain mode. The note \code{eager-hard} (rows that the machine declined by design with the switch: 20) is gone from the manifest. \code{SHostileEagerTest} now asserts, in the fast group: no row of the family is declined in place under five switch sets; the machine's verdict is the official one on every row without the note; the modes \code{shared} and \code{deferred} give no verdict that a row does not allow; and the deferred mode still declines at least 18 rows that are decided in place.}

\p{\strong{What partitioning buys}: the seven rows on which \code{local} and \code{shared} differ are the rows where a plain answer of an earlier DECLARATION is read under the flag: \code{warm-pos-across-declarations-eager-bad}, \code{e1-warm-plain-benign-then-eager-bad}, \code{oh-eager-lam-plain-then-eager-bad}, \code{mo3-decl-plain-then-eager-then-plain-bad}, \code{mo3-decl-answer-read-from-cache-then-eager-other-declaration-bad}, \code{mo3-susp-pair-plain-then-eager-other-declaration-bad}, \code{mo3-proofpos-pair-in-definitions-eager-bad}.}

\p{\strong{Seeded faults} (in a review copy, never in the patch; the eager family under three switch sets): without the search by expression (rule 5) \code{mo3-binder-inner-closed-between-other-records-ok} is rejected; without the plain comparison in the K rule (rule 6) \code{e1-skip-krule-plain-redex-then-eager-ok} is rejected, and \code{krule-eager-alone-bad} gets the official verdict - the trade of section 10, item 1; without the run alone (rule 7) the two rows of section 5.3 are rejected; if a comparison does not inherit the mark of the comparisons it asked, 13 verdicts move to an accept; if reading a local entry does not mark the reader, \code{mo3-decl-answer-read-from-cache-then-eager-other-declaration-bad} is accepted; without the mark before the same-head probe, 6 verdicts of the default set move to an accept; with the shortcuts of plain mode under the bit, 27. Every fault is seen by a row, and \code{SHostileEagerTest} fails on each of them.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.3 A wrong rejection of a first version, found by a row of this package}

\p{The rows \code{\verb~|mo3-decl-checked-term-reused-*~}: a definition whose value holds the open term \code{\verb~|(fun (h : X = Y p) => h) (Eq.refl X)~}, whose check compares \code{\verb~|X = X~} with \code{\verb~|X = Y p~} in plain mode; then a theorem that contains the same term under its own binder and needs \code{\verb~|X = Y p~} under the flag. The official checker accepts: it checks the term again in the theorem, and the success cache answers the eager comparison. A first version of the mechanism rejected: the machine knew the term as checked (the table of a warm session, and the shared check facts of the switch \code{pchk}) and never made the plain comparison in the theorem, the local entry of the definition's declaration was not read, and the eager comparison computed false. The published build accepts the export, through the entry of the earlier declaration. It was reported on the team's board when it was seen and nothing went further until the row was an agreement in every switch set and mode; rule 7 is the repair. The same cause inside one declaration - the type of a theorem and its value are checked under two instances of the binder, and the term is checked once - is section 5.4.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.4 Tried and given up}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Local entries that end with their binders.} A stamp per binder opening and a walk for a rebound variable made an entry end when a variable of its pair was bound again, as an official cache entry does. It gave the official verdict on the three binder rows of section 10 - and rejected \code{mo3-proofpos-pair-eager-same-declaration-ok} (official: accept) under the switch sets that check a theorem's type and its value under two instances of the binder (no switch but \code{eager}; step 1 with \code{eager}): the official kernel checks the applications again under the second instance and refills its cache, the machine's table of checked terms answers for the first. A local entry may not end earlier than the machine's memory of what it has checked; inside a declaration that is the declaration.}
\li{\strong{A mark that a suspended comparison loses.} A version in which a comparison that was suspended to the frame stack lost the enclosing comparison's mark, and everything in progress counted as marked from then on, was correct and cheap - and made the pair entries depend on the host depth bound, which the convention part of the machine's gate forbids and found (86 of 234 files with a difference between the bounds). The mark now travels in a frame.}
\li{\strong{A mark of the declaration} instead of a mark of the comparison (everything written after the first marked point of a declaration is local): 9 to 26 \% of the pair entries of a corpus were local and con-leche needed 1.9 \% more ticks than with shared entries. With the mark of the comparison 0.2 to 2.6 \% of the entries are local and the cost is below 0.05 \% (section 6).}
\li{\strong{The shortcuts of plain mode under the bit.} They cost nothing to remove there (identical tick counts on the eight corpora) and with them the machine accepted \code{mo3-proofpos-pair-in-definitions-eager-bad} through a skipped proof position.}
}
}
}

