\p{\strong{The cost of exactness in ticks is negative}: the machine does 0.02 to 1.25 \% fewer ticks than the published build, because the second runs in a fresh session and the probes are gone. \strong{The cost of partitioning} is the column \code{local} against \code{shared}: -0.03 to +0.04 \%. A local entry is read as absent by a later declaration, which then repeats the comparison; that is what the last column counts.}

\p{\strong{The deferred mode of the patched build is the published mechanism to the tick}: with \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-mode=deferred~} every counter of the machine that both builds have is identical to the published build's on the eight corpora (1,018 counters compared, 0 different).}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. The guard}

\p{[The rule for a default](dec-YP0N): the geometric mean of the warm passes of the eight corpora goes down and no corpus is more than 3 \% slower, over at least three interleaved rounds, on the executable (the target) and on the JVM launcher (which must not regress). Three variants, interleaved round by round in one window per launcher: the published build; the patched build (\code{local}, its default); the patched build with \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-mode=shared~}. Five rounds on the five short corpora, three on the three large ones. The windows, in UTC (the night that followed 2026-11-25): the executable 23:56 to 00:08 and 00:10 to 00:30, the JVM launcher 00:56 to 01:09 and 01:26 to 01:40, and the executable on Mathlib once more, 01:40 to 01:48. The repeat was needed because of an error of my own: during rounds 1 and 2 of Mathlib in the second window my accept and differential runs stood on the measurement cores (section 8), and the passes took 19 to 27 s where a clean one takes 12 to 13 s. The three rounds of that corpus were made again and the Mathlib row of the executable is the repeated window; the one clean round of the first window had given 13,077 / 12,956 / 12,946 ms. The cores outside the windows carried the work of other packages (load average 10 to 36 at the start of each corpus), and the kernel's count of compaction stalls did not move during any window. The CPU column is the CPU time of all threads for the median later pass, averaged over the rounds; peak RSS is the largest resident set of any process of the variant.}

