\p{\strong{Both launchers pass the rule}: the geometric mean goes down (0.996 on the executable, 0.993 on the JVM launcher) and no corpus is more than 3 \% slower (the largest ratios: 1.011 on con-leche for the executable; 1.008 on Std and on con-leche for the JVM launcher). \strong{The honest reading is "no change"}: every ratio lies inside the spread of the rounds of its own row, and the CPU time of a pass moves by -0.6 to +0.7 \% on the executable and by -2.2 to +2.5 \% on the JVM launcher. The mechanism was not built to be faster; it follows the official procedure and costs nothing that the guard can see. con-leche is the one corpus above 1.000 on both launchers, by 1.1 and 0.8 \%, with 0.45 \% fewer ticks than the published build; that was not examined further. \strong{Partitioning costs nothing measurable either}: \code{shared} against \code{local} is 0.997 on the executable and 0.995 on the JVM launcher in the geometric mean, with corpora on both sides of 1.000 on each launcher (0.979 to 1.006 and 0.985 to 1.025), which is what the tick counts of section 6 say. So there is no speed to buy by giving up the seven hostile rows that the local entries decide (section 5.2), and \code{local} is the default. \strong{The peak resident set is unchanged}: within 30 MiB of the published build on every corpus of the executable (Mathlib 4,061 and 4,067 MiB), within 51 MiB on the JVM launcher (Mathlib 4,819 and 4,810 MiB). One observation from the discarded rounds: one process of the candidate peaked at 5,405 MiB in a pass of Mathlib that took 26 s, while its workers had no cores to themselves; the other five processes of the two disturbed rounds stayed at 3,952 to 4,064 MiB, and all nine processes of the repeated window at 3,936 to 4,067 MiB. It was not examined further.}
}

